"A layered architecture diagram assigns software responsibilities to ordered layers and makes permitted dependency directions explicit.\n\nUse when: Review dependency rules across presentation, application, domain, and persistence. Explain which responsibilities belong together during refactoring.\n\nAvoid when: Physical server placement is the principal concern.\n\nVisual grammar: Horizontal bands encode logical layers, with modules inside their owning band. Arrows mean depends on or calls, as defined by the legend. Exceptions to the layer rule use labeled edges rather than color alone.\n\nBuild: Name layers by responsibility and declare whether layer skipping is permitted. Place representative modules inside padded bands. Draw dependencies consistently downward, separating runtime calls from source dependencies. Route exceptional edges outside bands and reserve a clean header strip.\n\nCheck: Every dependency follows the declared rule or has an explicit exception. Logical layers are not accidentally presented as separate machines. Dependency evidence identifies current code versus proposed structure. Band borders and edge labels remain clear of module text.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming more layers automatically improve architecture. Mixing invocation direction with compile-time dependency direction.\n\nExample: Checkout UI uses Application Services, which use Domain Rules; persistence implements a domain-facing interface.\n\nAlternatives: Ports and adapters diagram, Module dependency graph."
